LOGBOOK.md:1154:- EVIDENCE: The public macOS XCFramework exports only `libssh2_session_rekey`, `libssh2_keepalive_config`, and `libssh2_keepalive_send` among relevant symbols. `keepalive.c:52-96` contains no reply wait/state; inbound server rekey is private at `packet.c:1336-1369` and `transport.c:407-425,1039-1048`. Trace callbacks are unavailable in non-debug builds and are not a supported protocol API.
LOGBOOK.md:1155:- ROUTING: Do not report these gates green or build a mock-only partial adapter. Recommend a separately reviewed minimal fork extension exposing typed server-KEX lifecycle plus a nonblocking reply-correlated global-request/keepalive API; alternatives are explicitly failing the libssh2 candidate or revising the mandatory contract. Exact options and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_server-rekey-keepalive-blocker.md`.
LOGBOOK.md:1157:### 1115 — Minimal libssh2 public client-rekey fork clears the adapter blocker (TASK-260720-3vwls7)
LOGBOOK.md:1158:- DECISION: Pinned libssh2 `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` carries one allowlisted two-file patch: `include/libssh2.h` publicly declares `libssh2_session_rekey`, and `src/session.c` adds a null-checked, nonblocking-friendly `BLOCK_ADJUST` wrapper over the existing `ssh2_kex_exchange(session, 1, &session->startup_key_state)`. No crypto, algorithm, packet, channel, or private-header surface changed.
LOGBOOK.md:1160:- VERIFICATION: The public-header-only C integration test drove the wrapper through three `EAGAIN` results, observed client-initiated NEWKEYS installation, then forced OpenSSH `RekeyLimit` server-initiated NEWKEYS and proved post-rekey channels remained usable. Fork verify/rekey/source-tamper gates, Swift public-module packaging test, native seam tests, strict Swift format, 62 core tests, and `swift build` pass; `ReluxTunnelCore` remains free of libssh2/OpenSSL imports.
LOGBOOK.md:1162:### 1030 — libssh2 adapter stops on absent public client-rekey API (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
LOGBOOK.md:1163:- BLOCKER: Exact pinned archive `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` reproduced SHA-256 `744ba3e9…c87c`. Public `include/libssh2.h` has no rekey operation; initial `libssh2_session_handshake` calls private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=0)`, while only inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` reaches private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=1)` from `src/packet.c`.
LOGBOOK.md:1164:- IMPACT: Mandatory byte/time/explicit client rekey and `SSHTransport.requestRekey` cannot be implemented through the pinned public API. Calling the private symbol, exporting it in-task, replaying handshake, or reporting success without KEX would violate the task's no-patch/no-hidden-fallback boundary. Product code and partial packaging stopped before implementation.
LOGBOOK.md:1165:- ROUTING: Recommend recording libssh2 red/failed at this pin. The alternative is a separately authorized, audited libssh2 fork/public rekey hook (or a future upstream pin followed by full re-audit). Exact evidence and tradeoffs are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_libssh2-rekey-blocker.md`.
LOGBOOK.md:1176:- FINDING: Snapshot lifecycle is sticky-correct: `SSHConnectionStateMachine`'s own snapshot resets to nil on transition to `userAuthentication`/`active`, but `NIOSSHHandler` re-reads it after every processed message while the KEX state machine still holds `NegotiationResult`, and only overwrites on non-nil (`NIOSSHHandler.swift:684`). Consequence: during a rekey the handler snapshot updates when the new `NegotiationResult` is computed (KEXINIT processing), slightly before newKeys activate.
LOGBOOK.md:1205:- FINDING: During any KEX (client- or server-initiated) upstream's state machine rejects outbound `channelData` with `protocolViolation` (`SSHConnectionStateMachine.swift:1060-1076`), failing the child write promise. Pre-existing upstream behavior, unchanged by the fork — but automatic byte/time rekey makes the collision window likely under bulk traffic. The NIOSSH adapter (TASK-260715-1af33i) MUST gate/queue child writes on `rekeySnapshot.keyExchangeInProgress` / `NIOSSHRekeyStartedEvent`→`NIOSSHRekeySucceededEvent`; the fork exposes exactly this observability for the contract's "writes MAY suspend" clause.
LOGBOOK.md:1206:- FINDING: `NIOSSHChannelWindowConfiguration` invariant is `remaining + buffered + delivered == initialWindowSize`, so remaining credit never grows past `initialWindowSize` even when `maximumWindowSize` is larger, and a `windowAdjustmentThreshold > initialWindowSize` (allowed by preconditions, which only check ≤ max) permanently withholds WINDOW_ADJUST — a stalled-channel footgun. Adapter policy must keep threshold ≤ initial (≤ half is sane). Cap semantics remain contract-correct: `remainingBefore + adjustment ≤ cap` always holds.
LOGBOOK.md:1207:- NOTE: `.serverInitiated` rekey reason semantically means "remote-initiated" (a server-role handler labels client-triggered KEX the same way); cosmetic for the client-only tunnel. `requestRekey` in a non-active state fails the promise and also fires `errorCaught` — callers should not treat pre-active requests as recoverable no-ops.
LOGBOOK.md:1209:### 0850 — Minimal ReluxNIOSSH window/rekey fork is reviewable (TASK-260715-nzdzv3)
LOGBOOK.md:1210:- DECISION: The source fork is an independent SwiftPM package at `Dependencies/ReluxNIOSSH`, based byte-for-byte on SwiftNIO SSH `0.14.1` / `31cdc3c` before a 16-file allowlisted delta. The `NIOSSH` product/module and upstream 16 MiB/no-auto-rekey defaults remain source-compatible; `ReluxTunnelCore` has no dependency on the fork.
LOGBOOK.md:1211:- IMPLEMENTATION: Child initial window/cap/adjustment policy is set through a normal pre-open channel option and observed through snapshots/events; returned credit is earned only after pipeline delivery. Client manual, per-direction protected-byte, and injected-monotonic-time rekeys share the existing KEX state machine, coalesce concurrent reasons/promises, reset generation counters on success, and observe server-initiated KEX without product reflection or a test-only API.
LOGBOOK.md:1215:- DECISION: Both engine adapters use one serialized, type-erased `ReluxTunnelCore` boundary with mandatory raw host-key policy evaluation before credential access, bounded partial writes, independent byte-stream halves, capped observable per-channel receive credit, automatic byte/time plus explicit/server rekey, keepalive, stable errors, schema-v1 metrics/events, and privacy-safe opaque lane identity. ADR-014 remains open; unsupported mandatory behavior is a red matrix row, never a candidate-specific skip.
LOGBOOK.md:1217:- VERIFICATION: The contract defines every operation, state, timeout/cancellation scope, error, metric, privacy rule, and E-ALGO/E-HOSTAUTH/E-CHANNELS/E-BACKPRESSURE/E-WINDOW/E-REKEY/E-KEEPALIVE/E-CANCEL/E-SCALE/E-ERRORS/E-METRICS-PRIVACY/E-INJECTION row. Three task-scoped PlantUML sources pass syntax checking and their lifecycle, pre-auth host verification, and channel/window/rekey SVG renders were visually inspected.
LOGBOOK.md:1220:- MILESTONE: Audit accepted → `done`. Every load-bearing claim reproduced from upstream: tag→commit pins for both candidates plus all six transitive deps (ls-remote), all three archive SHA-256 and both license hashes (fresh downloads), 16 MiB window `SSHChannelMultiplexer.swift:211`, internal `_rekey()` `NIOSSHHandler.swift:511`, libssh2 window APIs and direct-tcpip hardcode `src/channel.c:384,457`, unexported reexchange `src/packet.c:1369`, version-macro anomaly, six CVE fix commits confirmed ancestors of `a343024`, GHSA advisories via API, exactly 679 libssh2 commits/12mo.
LOGBOOK.md:1226:- FINDING: SwiftNIO SSH hardcodes each child receive-window target to 16 MiB and exposes client `_rekey()` only internally. libssh2 exposes generic per-channel initial/remaining/adjust APIs, but its direct-tcpip helper fixes the 2 MiB default and it likewise has no public client-rekey API or automatic byte/time threshold.
LOGBOOK.md:1227:- DECISION: ADR-014 remains open. Both candidates retain identical host-key, window, sustained 5 GiB rekey, scale (through 1,000 channels), cancellation, algorithm, extension-build, memory, and physical-size gates. Unknowns are named experiments in `.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md`; no missing capability was waived.
